  the vast window of the sea.

  In his sixties, Marcus stopped expecting visitors. The occasional tour group would arrive in summer, clustered and
  camera-ready, and he'd show them up the stairs, watching their wonder at the view. After they left, he'd dust the
  gallery and return to his solitude.

  On a November evening, a letter arrived notifying him of his retirement. The lighthouse would be fully automated; his
  services were no longer required. He was seventy-two. He read the letter three times, then climbed the stairs one
  final time as an official keeper.

  The sun was setting orange and violent, the way it did in late autumn. Below, the rocks waited, patient and
  unchanging. Marcus looked at the light, freshly polished and gleaming, ready to spin through another night without
  him.

  He thought about all the ships that had passed, all the people who'd never known his name, all the nights his beam had
  become their salvation. He thought about his wife, long remarried, and the life he'd chosen not to live.

  Marcus descended the stairs slowly, his hand trailing the iron rail. At the bottom, he didn't lock the door behind
  him. He simply walked away, leaving the light to tend itself, as it had learned to do long ago.

  It still burns every night, bright and certain, waiting for someone to notice it's always been keeping the lighthouse,
  not the other way around.

